<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    ============================================================================
     HOW LONG A SCREEN TAKES TO SLIDE IN, ON ANDROID
    ============================================================================
     react-native-screens does not expose this. `animationDuration` on the
     navigator is documented as iOS only, and only for slide_from_bottom,
     fade_from_bottom, fade and simple_push. Ours is slide_from_right, so that
     prop would have been a line of code that nothing reads on either platform.

     What actually sets it is four animation resources inside the library, and
     all four ask for @android:integer/config_mediumAnimTime, which is 400.

     Android merges the app's resources over a library's when the names match,
     so declaring those same four names in android/app/src/main/res/anim
     replaces them without patching node_modules. Those four files point here,
     so the number lives once.

     300 rather than 250: "hafif daha hizli" was the ask. Android's own scale
     is 200 short, 400 medium, and 300 is the step between them rather than a
     number chosen by feel.

     iOS is untouched and cannot be touched the same way. There,
     slide_from_right is UINavigationController's own push and UIKit owns its
     timing.
    ============================================================================
-->
<resources>
    <integer name="rns_push_duration">300</integer>
</resources>
